    "math_academy_id": "q-142069",
    "knowledge_point_id": "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46",
    "knowledge_point": "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/8dbc36f0b404c1704e9add4b668b257703d6d163f825695b9404a33633da3ea7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)>0?$",
    "worked_solution": "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=a$ and $x=e$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/7f42ed4bdb45d14497c70b0e2e98af95442d9bf8e6aaa577766dec1c5df2b31f.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=a$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave  down ($⌢$) at $x=a$ only.",
